{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 480,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to the episode, I just wanted \nto let you know we have a brand new merch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "store with brand new merch. There’s \nmore info at the end of the episode!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’ve signed up for a cloninmixag experiment. \nGotta pay off them student loans, right? The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "researchers put you to sleep and place you in \ntheir cloning machine. It clones you at a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subatomic level, creating two perfectly copies. As  they had previously informed you, they even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24320,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take one of your clones (still asleep) to a room \nin LA and the other to a room in NY. As you wake  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up, you remember everything about the experiment \nup until the point you were put to sleep. A  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "researcher in the room with you then asks you\n“What is the probability that we are in LA?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41640,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You probably reply that there’s a \n50% chance you’re in LA. After all,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45840,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you know there are two copies of you but \nyou have no way to tell which one you are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50840,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so what if we split you into countless clones \ntraveling the many quantum timelines predicted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the Many Worlds interpretation of quantum \nmechanics. Can we figure out the probability that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61120,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’re in one particular timeline? It turns out we \ncan, and in the process derive the probabilities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68280,
    "dDurationMs": 5966,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for measurements of the quantum wavefunction \n– that is, to derive the famous Born Rule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79300,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Flip a coin and cover it without looking. You \nknow it’s definitely either heads or tails  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84560,
    "dDurationMs": 5705,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before you look, and if it’s a fair coin \nthe probability of it being either is 50%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90265,
    "dDurationMs": 6495,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now if this was a quantum coin, it’s still 50-50 \nthat you’ll see heads or tails when you look,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96760,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but before you look the coin is \nnot in either definite state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101120,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, quantum mechanics describes the \nworld as wavefunctions—fuzzy wuzzy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distributions of possibility that evolve \naccording to the Schrodinger equation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before the quantum coin is revealed, its \nwavefunction is neither heads nor tails,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115240,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but a quantum superposition of both. What does \nthat even mean? I don’t know. No one knows. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123280,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we do know that we have to describe the quantum \nworld in terms of superpositions to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get the right answers when we calculate \nquantum processes. Upon observing the coin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this superposition evaporates and the coin is \nonce more definitely heads or definitely tails.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mystery of what happens when we go from a \nsuperposition to a definite state is known as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Measurement Problem, and it’s arguably the \nmost mysterious outstanding problem in physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150000,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The different interpretations of quantum mechanics \nare really about solving the measurement problem.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most mainstream answer—the Copenhagen \ninterpretation—argues that upon measurement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160000,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all but one of the superposition \nof states vanish, leaving only the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state we observe. We say the wavefunction \ncollapses. Then we have Pilot Wave Theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which states that there’s an underlying sort \nof labeling system—specks of reality we’ll call  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177400,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corpuscles—that defines which of the multiplicity \nof states in a superposition are actually real,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184720,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relegating the rest of the branches to a sort of \nempty, ghostly state. Both Copenhagen and Pilot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wave need to add some additional mechanics \nto the Schrodinger equation in response to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196040,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Measurement Problem. And then of course \nthere’s the Many Worlds Interpretation, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200800,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states that the Schrodinger equation is the entire \nstory—there’s no collapse, no labeling of the one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real branch. Rather, the wavefunction evolves only \naccording to the Schrodinger equation forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211640,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our previous episode in this series \nwe talked about how Pilot Wave Theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215320,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may just be Many Worlds in disguise. But \nI promised we’d see if Many Worlds can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219080,
    "dDurationMs": 864,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take us further. For example, does \nit solve the Measurement Problem?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219944,
    "dDurationMs": 4056,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, if Many Worlds tells \nus that wavefunction doesn’t collapse,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why don’t we observe superpositions of \nmacroscopic states—like the alive and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dead cat in Schrodinger’s thought experiment? \nWell, Many Worlds would say it’s because we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234600,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren’t viewing the wavefunction from outside. \nRather, our subjective experience is embedded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within a branch of the wavefunction where \nwe can observe only a single outcome—say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heads—which means there’s another you \nin another branch observing tails."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251760,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether or not this is true, it’s \ncompelling enough for many physicists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254800,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take it very seriously. The problem \nis that on the surface Many Worlds seems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259400,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unverifiable. There’s no way for us to \never interact with these other branches."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there may be a way to at least improve our \nconfidence in a theory without a direct test,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271200,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s by seeing if the theory offers an \nexplanation for something else confusing in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics. And there is one more thing \nthat we’ve just sort of accepted since the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beginning of quantum mechanics but never fully \nexplained, and that’s the Born rule. The Born  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288440,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rule is how we find the probability of getting a \nparticular result when we make a measurement. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probability is just the square of the wavefunction \nfor the property we’re measuring. The Born rule is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taken as a starting axiom in standard quantum \nmechanics. But if an interpretation could tell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305440,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us why the Born rule works the way it does, \nthen that would be a point in its favour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310920,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, today I’m going to present an argument that \nthe Born rule falls naturally out of Many Worlds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but not quite so naturally \nout of other interpretations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321880,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by looking at our \nquantum coin with some proper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325560,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum language. We can write its pre-measurement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction as a linear combination of \na wavefunction for heads and for tails."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The alpha and beta are coefficients that represent \nthe weight or strength of each of the basis  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341960,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states. We can think of the wavefunction as an \narrow—what we call a state vector—pointing in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348480,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some direction indicating how much “heads-ness” \nor “tails-ness” this wavefunction has. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way the Schrodinger equation works, the \nlength of this arrow is always the same,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358360,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so good ol’ Pythagorus tells us that its length \nis the sum of the squares of the coefficients.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For convenience we always set the length of \nthis vector to equal one. So in this case we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372400,
    "dDurationMs": 880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alpha and beta are 1/2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For our quantum coin, the Born rule tells us that \nthe probability that we’ll measure heads or tails  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379360,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the square of the coefficient associated \nwith that state. Alpha-squared it’s heads,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384200,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beta-squared it’s tails. If this is \na fair coin then the probabilities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387960,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the same—both alpha-squared and \nbeta-squared need to equal a half,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392000,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so alpha and beta are the square root of a half. \nOur fair-quantum-coin wavefunction looks like this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we’re committed to the coefficients defining \nthe probabilities—which honestly we don’t have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406240,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a good reason for just yet—then the Born rule is \nthe only way for those probabilities to be equal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for equal coefficients. But we can do better \nthan this—we can show that the probabilities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "HAVE to be governed by the coefficients and that \nthe Born rule applies even if they aren’t equal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425160,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at what happens when we measure our \nquantum coin to see if we can find the place  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430240,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where coefficients turn into probabilities. \nTo do this we need to place ourselves into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435720,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction and track the series of \ninteractions between the flipped quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coin and our awareness of the result \nof that flip. Because the universe is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamentally quantum, the classicalness emerges  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448800,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the combination of an extremely \nlarge number of quantum interactions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453000,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s write the wavefunction of the universe \nright after the coin flips but before the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458400,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result is known. We include the state of the \nmeasurement apparatus by just multiplying its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own wavefunction into the coin’s superposition. \nAs well as the observer’s wavefunction, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction of the rest of the universe—E \nfor the environment or for everything else."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476480,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As information about the flipped coin travels \nout, each of the miriad individual quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481960,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constituents of these external entities \neventually becomes entangled with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486080,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flipped coin. Each quantum element of the \nsurrounding world enters its superposition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490920,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the two possible states—having interacted \nwith a result of tails and having interacted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494080,
    "dDurationMs": 9240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a result of heads. Assuming we don’t collapse \nany wavefunctions, then the measurement device,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503320,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the observer, and everything else enters \nthe horrifically complex superposition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509040,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Measurement Problem is really the \nquestion of why we never observe such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512840,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "macroscopic superpositions, but rather only \none the definite results within. Many Worlds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "claims to solve this by stating that we are \nnot outside the superposition. We’re here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or here, inside one of the branches. We see \nthe superposition component that we are inside,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not the whole thing. The many quantum \nstates that make up your brain and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537280,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hence your conscious experience \nsplit with the rest of the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many Worlds can’t say why we observe one \nspecific state over another state—rather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it tells us why we observe only a \nsingle state. And it can also tell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551240,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us why we measure the probabilities \nthat we do—why we get the Born rule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556320,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like in our first thought experiment where a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558680,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clone could state a probability of \nfinding themselves in LA versus NY,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564400,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps we can figure out the probability of \nfinding ourselves in one “world” versus another."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In their reasoning, our clone applied \nsomething called the Principle of Indifference,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which states that “in the absence of any relevant \nevidence, agents should distribute their credence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581000,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or 'degrees of belief', equally among all the \npossible outcomes under consideration.” So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588160,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two outcomes, no evidence to distinguish them,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591360,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their probability, evenly divided should add \nto one—ergo 50-50 LA versus NY. If there was a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "third clone sent to Buenos Aires then the \nprobabilities would by ⅓ for each city."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605400,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s do our quantum coin experiment again, \nbut now asking our observer to close their eyes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until the coin, the measurement device, and \nthe environment know the result of the flip.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615440,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s going to be some little neural circuit \nin the brain of the observer corresponding to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knowing the result of the coin flip. We define \nthe wavefunction of that circuit as the observer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626440,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it is identical after the flip but before \nbecoming aware of the result of the flip."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the observer asks “what’s the probability \nthat when I open my eyes I see heads versus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639440,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tails?” We have two possible worlds, so \nis it 50-50? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would be true if alpha and beta are \nthe same. It’s possible to show very rigorously that when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "branches have equal coefficients the Principle of \nIndifference applies and the credence an observer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should have of being in the tails branch \nor the heads branch is the same—and I’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663120,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "put a link to that proof in the description. \nThe probability of you observing one of any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of equally-weighted outcomes is one over \nthe number of outcomes—one over the number of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“worlds”--which is the coefficient of your world \nsquared. And that is, of course, the Born rule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if the coefficients for the \nquantum coin are different? Say, and squqre  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "root(1/3) and square root(2/3)? If \nthere are still only two worlds, heads and tails"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694200,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it seems intuitive that the probability \nshould still be 50-50. Why should the coefficients  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699280,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even matter? But the Born rule says that \nthe probabilities are ⅓ and ⅔ for heads versus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tails in this case. So does that mean Many \nWorlds is inconsistent with the Born rule?\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, no. There is a way to go from quantum \nstates to the correct probabilities even in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717960,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the case of unequal coefficients without ever \nassuming the Born rule. We can still count worlds. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725400,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we do that, let’s count cards. \nI deal you 6 cards, and only one of them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a joker. I shuffle and deal out \ntwo stacks of three—one for you and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one for me. What’s the probability that the \njoker is in your stack? You know it’s 50%,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you know that because you intuitively \napply the principle of indifference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748400,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just to be pedantic, let’s formally justify the \nprinciple of indifference in this case. If I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "swap the stacks everything looks the same, \nso your credence that you have the joker  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758360,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shouldn’t change. But also your credence that \nthe joker is in your new stack after the swap  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764640,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be the same that it’s in my stack before \nthe swap because they’re the same stack. So the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probability of the joker being in your stack \npre-swap is the same as it being in my stack  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pre-swap. Those two probabilities have \nto add to one, so they’re each a half."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782440,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what happens if, after dealing, I take move \ncard from your stack to mine. Now you have 2 cards  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I have 4. Now what’s the probability \nthat you have the joker? The Principle of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Indifference can’t be applied directly because \nif we swap the stacks things look different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799240,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a way around this. I \njust split my deck in 2. Now we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803920,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have 3 stacks of 2 cards. Any swapping \nof these stacks looks exactly the same,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the principle of indifference tells us that \nthere’s equal probability that the joker is in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815000,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any one of them. So the probability that the \njoker is in your one stack is ⅓. It’s also ⅓  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821440,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it’s in either particular one of my stacks, \nand ⅔ overall that it’s in one of my two stacks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we could have got the same answer \njust with frequentist arguments about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832360,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there being one joker in 6 cards \nof which you have 2 cards. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s equivalent to the swapping \nargument with stack sizes of one card."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842200,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do we apply this to Many Worlds? \nUnlike with the cards we don’t know,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847920,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps can’t know, how many discrete worlds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are. But that doesn’t mean we \ncan’t divide the worlds into stacks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857680,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s go back to the case of our unfair quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860160,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coin. Leaving out the measurement \ndevice and the environment for now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864760,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction after flipping the coin and \nprior to opening our eyes looks like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869840,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know from the principle of indifference \nthat these two branches should NOT have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873800,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equal probability. So let’s break up \nthe second term just like we split  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our stacks of cards. Don’t worry just yet if \nwe’re even allowed to do this. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say that the tails state is really \ntwo equal sub-states |T_1\u003e and |T_2\u003e"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891520,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The coefficient of sqrt(½) comes from \nthinking of these sub-states as orthogonal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vectors that make up the tails state, \nwith lengths given by Pythagorus' theorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903120,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could do any number of substates,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but two gives us the right coefficient \nso that when we expand this out…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All coefficients are now equal. Now we can apply \nthe principle of indifference to say that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917520,
    "dDurationMs": 9640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probability of all these branches is ⅓. That’s ⅓ \nfor heads and for either of the tails substates,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927160,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or ⅔ for the sum of tails substates. And there’s \nyour Born rule—the coefficients squared are the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probabilities. And that’s just from the \nPrinciple of indifference forcing you to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939360,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equally weight your credence about which branch \nyou’re in when the coefficients are equal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, some very serious questions immediately come to mind. Like, are we really allowed to break up any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state in any way we want? Are those divided \ncomponents also valid quantum states? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remember that we never observe a quantum \nobject directly. We are observing the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "macroscopic effect of that quantum object \non a measurement device and embedded in an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968560,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "environment. The full wavefunction of the \nquantum coin in the world looks like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The measurement device and the environment \nare made of countless quantum objects,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each of which can also exist in \nmultiple states. So after the coin flip,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984600,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are many, many states in the device and \nthe environment that all correspond to heads,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990880,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and many also to tails. No matter the coefficients \nof the quantum event, we can split the states of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world so they have equal coefficients between \nthose states. Then we can apply the principle of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "indifference to say there’s equal probability of \nbeing in one of these “stacks of worlds”. Then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just sum the stacks that are associated with a \nparticular outcome of a quantum event. You’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015800,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always find the resulting probability is equal to \nthe square of the coefficient for that outcome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should also ask: Is this argument is really \nexclusively valid for Many Worlds? Can we use  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029200,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this to derive the Born Rule for Copenhagen \nor Pilot Wave Theory for example? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some parts are valid for other interpretations. \nFor example, as long as we imagine that the state  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re measuring is in some way course-grained—so, \ncomposed of other, finer states, then we can split  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up the state so that the overall superposition \nis a chain of states with equal coefficients. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053640,
    "dDurationMs": 9080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s not then clear what it means to apply \nthe principle of indifference in the other interpretation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Copenhagen, we have to say that the \nlikelihood of branches collapsing is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067440,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "governed by the coefficient of the branch, \nsuch that equally weighted branches have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072520,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equal likelihood of surviving the collapse. Same \nwith Pilot Wave Theory, but with respect to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "locations of the corpuscles rather than branch \ncollapse. If we’re happy with that then sure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can apply the principle of indifference \nto get the Born rule. But we still have this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088240,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "axiomatic requirement linking the coefficients \nto some mysterious quantum event that’s not part  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Schrodinger equation, even if it’s a \nmore subtle axiom than the full Born rule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Many Worlds, the application of the principle \nof indifference is the correct way to decide  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1105800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which branch—or family of branches—we belong \nto, and so we can say that the Born rule does  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "follow naturally from this interpretation. \nMany-Worlds is rather unique amongst the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117080,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "popular interpretations because it has \na direct path to finding probabilities,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than having to bake it into the theory \nby axiom. We just need to ask what is the chance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127800,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of finding ourselves in this or that part of the \nwavefunction of an endlessly branching Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135720,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone! We wanted to let you know that \nwe have a brand new merch store. We have new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1140240,
    "dDurationMs": 9440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "t-shirts to signal your nerd pride. New hoodies and a beanie to protect you from the endless cold of the dying universe. And a Full Standard Model Lagrangian mug  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to help you ponder the full nature of reality while getting your morning buzz. And for the month  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154560,
    "dDurationMs": 5298,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of December everything in the store is 15% \noff. There’s a link in the description!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
